{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"1dfb8dd4648190e8","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is Amazon VPC?**|What is Amazon VPC?]]","x":-100,"y":0,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"27eff045af545f1d","type":"text","text":"`Amazon VPC` means `Amazon Virtual Private Cloud`.\nAmazon VPC lets you create your own private, controlled, and secure network inside AWS.  \nNormally, if you run servers in a company data center, you need to design the network yourself.\nFor example:\n```\nIP addresses\nSubnets\nRouting\nGateways\nSecurity\n```\nAmazon VPC gives you the same kind of control, but inside the AWS Cloud.\n**Simple idea**  \nA `VPC` is like your own private network inside AWS.  \nInside that network, you can run resources like:\n```\nEC2 servers\nDatabases\nLoad balancers\nContainers\n```\nYou control how those resources connect to each other and to the internet.  ","x":300,"y":0,"width":500,"height":675},
		{"id":"dc33116523649c11","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a VPC CIDR block?**|What is a VPC CIDR block?]]","x":-100,"y":690,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"f7563193de6d4ab0","type":"text","text":"When you create a VPC, you choose an IP address range.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis range is called a `CIDR block`.  \nThe CIDR block decides what private IP addresses can exist inside your VPC.  ","x":300,"y":690,"width":500,"height":234},
		{"id":"1b82c85584b44152","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What does `10.0.0.0/16` mean?**|What does `10.0.0.0/16` mean?]]","x":-100,"y":965,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"ef347fe8689a4d72","type":"text","text":"```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis means your VPC can use IP addresses from the `10.0.x.x` range.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.1.10\n10.0.2.25\n10.0.100.50\n```\nA `/16` CIDR block gives a large number of IP addresses.  ","x":300,"y":965,"width":500,"height":330},
		{"id":"3b5bff92561533a2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**Can you change the main VPC CIDR block later?**|Can you change the main VPC CIDR block later?]]","x":-100,"y":1355,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"a7b16c871e023967","type":"text","text":"After you create the VPC, the main CIDR block cannot be changed.  \nSo you should choose it carefully.  \nFor example, if you create your VPC with:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThat becomes the primary IP range for the VPC.  ","x":300,"y":1355,"width":500,"height":233},
		{"id":"d576916647ca741a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a subnet?**|What is a subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":1630,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1a28ee0de1e4ad1c","type":"text","text":"A `subnet` is a smaller network inside a VPC.  \nYou divide a VPC into subnets to organize resources.  \nFor example, you may create:\n```\nPublic subnet\nPrivate subnet\n```\nA public subnet can be used for web servers.  \nA private subnet can be used for databases.  \n**Example VPC and subnet design**  \nYour VPC may use:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen you divide it into smaller subnets:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24    Public subnet\n10.0.2.0/24    Private subnet\n```\nThe VPC is the big network.  \nThe subnets are smaller parts inside that network.  ","x":300,"y":1630,"width":500,"height":585},
		{"id":"cfdef20f6a2aaf3f","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a public subnet?**|What is a public subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":2275,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"22736dfc0ad0c6b7","type":"text","text":"A `public subnet` is a subnet that can connect to the internet through an `Internet Gateway`.  \nYou usually put internet-facing resources here.  \nFor example:\n```\nWeb server\nLoad balancer\nBastion host\n```","x":300,"y":2275,"width":500,"height":250},
		{"id":"cf156ffcfa6bb330","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a private subnet?**|What is a private subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":2585,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1f940d34fcf519ed","type":"text","text":"A `private subnet` is a subnet that is not directly reachable from the public internet.  \nYou usually put internal resources here.  \nFor example:\n```\nDatabase\nPrivate backend service\nInternal application server\n```\nPrivate resources can still access the internet through a `NAT Gateway`, but the internet cannot directly access them.  ","x":300,"y":2585,"width":500,"height":308},
		{"id":"596041e3d4ac68a6","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**Does a VPC belong to one AWS Region?**|Does a VPC belong to one AWS Region?]]","x":-100,"y":2920,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"3b11028ee0da5014","type":"text","text":"Yes.  \nA VPC belongs to one AWS Region.  \nFor example, if you create a VPC in the Singapore Region, it exists only in that Region.  \nYou can create multiple VPCs in the same Region.  ","x":300,"y":2920,"width":500,"height":144},
		{"id":"d25399ac71f8a3a9","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What does CIDR block size mean?**|What does CIDR block size mean?]]","x":-100,"y":3115,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"9a79a0db405facf0","type":"text","text":"The CIDR block size controls how many IP addresses your VPC can have.  \nFor example:\n```\n/16 means 65,536 IP addresses\n/28 means 16 IP addresses\n```\nA smaller number after `/` means a bigger network.  \nA bigger number after `/` means a smaller network.  ","x":300,"y":3115,"width":500,"height":259},
		{"id":"1fa83391258a1004","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**Why should VPC CIDR blocks not overlap?**|Why should VPC CIDR blocks not overlap?]]","x":-100,"y":3420,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"f74258010eb3e1bb","type":"text","text":"Your VPC CIDR block should not overlap with other networks.  \nThis is very important when you connect your VPC to another network.  \nFor example:\n```\nCompany office network\nAWS VPC\nVPN network\nAnother VPC\n```\nIf the IP ranges overlap, routing becomes confusing.  \n**Example of overlapping CIDR problem**  \nImagine your company network uses:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen your AWS VPC should not also use:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nBecause when traffic wants to go to:\n```\n10.0.1.20\n```\nThe system becomes confused.\n```\nIs 10.0.1.20 in the office network\\?**\n?\nOr is 10.0.1.20 inside the AWS VPC\\?**\n?\n```\nSo you should choose a different CIDR block for your VPC.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.10.0.0/16\n```\nor:\n```\n172.16.0.0/16\n```","x":300,"y":3420,"width":500,"height":1075},
		{"id":"d4eb2c4ba780e793","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What came before Amazon VPC?**|What came before Amazon VPC?]]","x":-100,"y":4555,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"0f6504974117356c","type":"text","text":"Amazon VPC came after Amazon EC2.  \nIn the past, AWS had two networking models:\n```\nEC2-Classic\nEC2-VPC\n```","x":300,"y":4555,"width":500,"height":195},
		{"id":"c32269d32f8bf639","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What was EC2-Classic?**|What was EC2-Classic?]]","x":-100,"y":4810,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"221ce6c2ac471074","type":"text","text":"`EC2-Classic` was the old AWS networking model.  \nIn this model, resources used a shared, flat network.  \nThat means EC2 instances were not running inside your own private VPC.  \nMany AWS customers used the shared AWS network model.  ","x":300,"y":4810,"width":500,"height":145},
		{"id":"6cbdc28bf662f367","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is EC2-VPC?**|What is EC2-VPC?]]","x":-100,"y":5005,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"69eefa845772fae8","type":"text","text":"`EC2-VPC` is the current AWS networking model.  \nIn this model, resources run inside isolated virtual networks.  \nThat virtual network is your VPC.  \nThis is more secure and gives you more control.  ","x":300,"y":5005,"width":500,"height":135},
		{"id":"4f915cbc9e9b3a03","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is the default VPC?**|What is the default VPC?]]","x":-100,"y":5200,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"edac3fe743575e2f","type":"text","text":"AWS creates a default VPC in each Region.  \nThe default VPC uses this CIDR block:\n```\n172.31.0.0/16\n```\nThe default VPC is useful for learning and quick testing.  \n**Should you use the default VPC for production?**  \n?  \nFor real production systems, it is usually better to create a custom VPC.  \nA custom VPC gives you better control over:\n```\nIP ranges\nSubnets\nRouting\nGateways\nSecurity\nNetwork architecture\n```\n**Important VPC components**  \nThere are four important VPC components:\n```\nSubnet\nRoute table\nInternet Gateway\nNAT Gateway\n```\nThese components help control how traffic moves inside and outside your VPC.  ","x":300,"y":5200,"width":500,"height":765},
		{"id":"9b892119c1a34ec1","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a subnet?**|What is a subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":6025,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"8bf3043ea62c9b25","type":"text","text":"A `subnet` is a smaller network inside a VPC.  \nYou use subnets to organize resources.  \nFor example:\n```\nPublic subnet for web servers\nPrivate subnet for databases\n```","x":300,"y":6025,"width":500,"height":220},
		{"id":"98ae9523fd1b05df","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a route table?**|What is a route table?]]","x":-100,"y":6305,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"4f1bda959656e55e","type":"text","text":"A `route table` is a set of rules that tells network traffic where to go.  \nFor example, a route table can say:\n```\nTraffic to the internet → Internet Gateway\nTraffic inside VPC → local route\nTraffic to private network → VPN Gateway\n```\nRoute tables control network paths.  ","x":300,"y":6305,"width":500,"height":258},
		{"id":"538e44bb83d2f56a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a gateway?**|What is a gateway?]]","x":-100,"y":6615,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"790016cbd34f4c68","type":"text","text":"A `gateway` is a connection point between networks.  \nIn AWS, gateways help your VPC connect to:\n```\nThe internet\nAnother private network\nAn on-premises network\n```","x":300,"y":6615,"width":500,"height":225},
		{"id":"6ea0e6b3b7943e60","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is an Internet Gateway?**|What is an Internet Gateway?]]","x":-100,"y":6900,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"cf0095599ea04ce7","type":"text","text":"An `Internet Gateway` is a VPC component that allows resources in public subnets to access the internet.  \nIt also allows internet traffic to reach public resources in your VPC.  \nFor example, a public EC2 web server needs an Internet Gateway to be reachable from the internet.  ","x":300,"y":6900,"width":500,"height":175},
		{"id":"7769d8b793807b81","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a NAT Gateway?**|What is a NAT Gateway?]]","x":-100,"y":7080,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"51f4435ec0072ebf","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` allows private resources to access the internet without allowing the internet to directly access them.  \nFor example, an EC2 instance in a private subnet may need to download software updates.  \nIt can use a NAT Gateway for outbound internet access.  \nBut people from the internet cannot directly connect to that private EC2 instance.  ","x":300,"y":7080,"width":500,"height":202},
		{"id":"582f351d7c1c0093","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a Virtual Private Gateway?**|What is a Virtual Private Gateway?]]","x":-100,"y":7275,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"3144d1aaa5198094","type":"text","text":"`Virtual Private Gateway`, or `VGW`, is a gateway used to connect a VPC to another private network.  \nIt is often used with a VPN connection to connect AWS to an on-premises network.  \nFor example:\n```\nCompany office network → VPN → Virtual Private Gateway → AWS VPC\n```","x":300,"y":7275,"width":500,"height":242},
		{"id":"ff09b635271f561c","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is IPv4?**|What is IPv4?]]","x":-100,"y":7525,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"62c28db73d034db5","type":"text","text":"`IPv4` is the older and most common IP address system.  \nExample IPv4 address:\n```\n192.168.1.1\n```\nMost common private network ranges use IPv4.  ","x":300,"y":7525,"width":500,"height":190},
		{"id":"cbf0b060081cdced","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is IPv6?**|What is IPv6?]]","x":-100,"y":7775,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"b49368ab5b9a2e49","type":"text","text":"`IPv6` is a newer IP address system with a much larger address space.  \nIPv6 addresses are longer than IPv4 addresses.  ","x":300,"y":7775,"width":500,"height":120},
		{"id":"19f62f4a35e15685","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a primary CIDR block?**|What is a primary CIDR block?]]","x":-100,"y":7955,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"13001a153b10c9b0","type":"text","text":"A `primary CIDR block` is the main IP address range assigned to a VPC when it is created.  \nExample:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis is the main private IP range for the VPC.  ","x":300,"y":7955,"width":500,"height":210},
		{"id":"9bd33dc8746989fc","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a CIDR block?**|What is a CIDR block?]]","x":-100,"y":8205,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d3d1e7122563eb5f","type":"text","text":"A `CIDR block` is an IP address range.  \nIt can be used by:\n```\nVPC\nSubnet\nNetwork\n```\nExample:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nor:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24\n```\n<!--SR:!2026-07-30,1,230-->","x":300,"y":8205,"width":500,"height":460},
		{"id":"ab09651bf2b85de9","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is an on-premises network?**|What is an on-premises network?]]","x":-100,"y":8725,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"84cfc32d7c0c51cc","type":"text","text":"An `on-premises network` is a company's own physical network outside the cloud.  \nFor example, your company may have:\n```\nOffice building\n├── Routers\n├── Switches\n├── Servers\n└── Computers\n```\nThis network is managed by the company, not by AWS.  ","x":300,"y":8725,"width":500,"height":310},
		{"id":"3613de1fac11a712","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is Amazon EC2?**|What is Amazon EC2?]]","x":-100,"y":9095,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d60f2ddaf7023ce3","type":"text","text":"`Amazon EC2` is a service that lets you create and run virtual servers in AWS.  \nAn EC2 instance is like a server in the cloud.  \nYou can install applications, databases, or backend services on it.  ","x":300,"y":9095,"width":500,"height":115},
		{"id":"03ca7f8ffb859a9b","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a flat network?**|What is a flat network?]]","x":-100,"y":9275,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"22c13d0eba112043","type":"text","text":"A `flat network` is a simple network without strong separation between different parts.  \nIn a flat network, many resources may exist in the same network area.  \nThis gives less control compared to a properly designed VPC with subnets and routing.  ","x":300,"y":9275,"width":500,"height":175},
		{"id":"b6bdc083585af571","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a shared network?**|What is a shared network?]]","x":-100,"y":9455,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"113290eee713d890","type":"text","text":"A `shared network` is a network used by many customers or systems together.  \nEC2-Classic used a shared network model in the past.  \nVPC is better because it gives each customer isolated network control.  ","x":300,"y":9455,"width":500,"height":145},
		{"id":"cfca1460601dd7cd","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is subnet sizing?**|What is subnet sizing?]]","x":-100,"y":9635,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d66f137812ecd31b","type":"text","text":"`Subnet sizing` means choosing how large a subnet should be.  \nYou choose the subnet size by selecting a CIDR block.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24\n```\nA `/24` subnet gives 256 IP addresses.  \nYou should choose subnet sizes carefully so you have enough IP addresses for your resources.  ","x":300,"y":9635,"width":500,"height":259},
		{"id":"af4105164781c832","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is external internet traffic?**|What is external internet traffic?]]","x":-100,"y":9935,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"2b49e2946c9ab61d","type":"text","text":"`External internet traffic` is traffic coming from outside AWS.  \nFor example:\n```\nUser browser → Internet → Your public web server in AWS\n```\nThis is inbound traffic from the public internet.  ","x":300,"y":9935,"width":500,"height":234},
		{"id":"f852f3c2e492f7e2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is internet-bound traffic?**|What is internet-bound traffic?]]","x":-100,"y":10185,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"bb749697c2988285","type":"text","text":"`Internet-bound traffic` is traffic going to the internet.  \nFor example:\n```\nPrivate EC2 instance → NAT Gateway → Internet\n```\nThis is outbound traffic from your VPC to the internet.  ","x":300,"y":10185,"width":500,"height":190},
		{"id":"c6b4345e91000184","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is network architecture?**|What is network architecture?]]","x":-100,"y":10435,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"12e291f4ff2e3a52","type":"text","text":"`Network architecture` is the overall design of how your network is organized.  \nIt includes:\n```\nVPCs\nSubnets\nRoute tables\nGateways\nSecurity groups\nCIDR blocks\nTraffic flow\n```\nGood network architecture makes your system more secure and easier to manage.  ","x":300,"y":10435,"width":500,"height":378},
		{"id":"ff742b70dddab92b","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a VPC component?**|What is a VPC component?]]","x":-100,"y":10865,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d28f1cf8412f62d6","type":"text","text":"A `VPC component` is a networking part inside or attached to a VPC.  \nExamples:\n```\nSubnet\nRoute table\nInternet Gateway\nNAT Gateway\nSecurity group\nNetwork ACL\n```","x":300,"y":10865,"width":500,"height":315},
		{"id":"2c4acfd443b4d773","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a private IP address?**|What is a private IP address?]]","x":-100,"y":11240,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"c560abf1a490387a","type":"text","text":"A `private IP address` is an internal IP address used inside a private network.  \nExample:\n```\n10.0.1.20\n```\nPrivate IP addresses are used for communication inside the VPC.  \nThey are not directly reachable from the public internet.  ","x":300,"y":11240,"width":500,"height":234},
		{"id":"38ffb293912dc8dd","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a public IP address?**|What is a public IP address?]]","x":-100,"y":11515,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"266ab06645cfc2e3","type":"text","text":"A `public IP address` is an IP address that can be used on the public internet.  \nIf an EC2 instance has a public IP address and the correct routing/security settings, people from the internet can reach it.  ","x":300,"y":11515,"width":500,"height":120},
		{"id":"6ecc6b8f4d4b59f2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is an Elastic IP address?**|What is an Elastic IP address?]]","x":-100,"y":11695,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"037d01cca7a7d3aa","type":"text","text":"An `Elastic IP address` is a static public IP address that you can allocate in AWS and attach to resources.  \nUnlike a normal public IP address, an Elastic IP can stay the same.  \nFor example, you can attach it to an EC2 instance or a NAT Gateway.\n<!--SR:!2026-07-30,1,230-->","x":300,"y":11695,"width":500,"height":355},
		{"id":"83c20171ca489618","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is source IP?**|What is source IP?]]","x":-100,"y":11890,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"49e73946e2ba9f5b","type":"text","text":"`Source IP` is the IP address where traffic comes from.  \nFor example:\n```\nUser IP → Web server\n```\nThe user's IP address is the source IP.  ","x":300,"y":11890,"width":500,"height":190},
		{"id":"13c4611e1ae59254","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is Network Address Translation?**|What is Network Address Translation?]]","x":-100,"y":12140,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d0495e87c04b3621","type":"text","text":"`Network Address Translation`, or `NAT`, means changing one IP address into another IP address when traffic moves between networks.  \nFor example, a private EC2 instance may use a NAT Gateway to access the internet.  \nThe internet sees the NAT Gateway's public IP address, not the private EC2 instance's private IP address.  ","x":300,"y":12140,"width":500,"height":202},
		{"id":"640a95090f5f9ee5","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is outbound traffic?**|What is outbound traffic?]]","x":-100,"y":12320,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"eaefdac52f046090","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is inbound traffic?**|What is inbound traffic?]]","x":-100,"y":12545,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"26875bf3050cd609","type":"text","text":"`Inbound traffic` is traffic coming into your VPC from the internet or another network.  \nExample:\n```\nInternet user → Public web server\n```","x":300,"y":12545,"width":500,"height":165},
		{"id":"8fcf5964f21e2a67","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What are data transfer rates?**|What are data transfer rates?]]","x":-100,"y":12770,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"f207db5a53ca9192","type":"text","text":"`Data transfer rates` are costs charged based on how much data moves in or out.  \nFor example, AWS may charge for data transfer from your VPC to the internet.  ","x":300,"y":12770,"width":500,"height":120},
		{"id":"c04df647c5e40be8","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What are bandwidth constraints?**|What are bandwidth constraints?]]","x":-100,"y":12950,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"0565755c6add04c3","type":"text","text":"`Bandwidth constraints` are limits on how much network traffic can pass through.  \nFor example, some resources or network paths may have limits on traffic speed or volume.  ","x":300,"y":12950,"width":500,"height":120},
		{"id":"b26e42522e278584","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a NAT Instance?**|What is a NAT Instance?]]","x":-100,"y":13130,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"b4b946583ba330b0","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Instance` is an EC2 instance manually configured to perform NAT.  \nYou manage it yourself.  \nCompared with a NAT Gateway, a NAT Instance gives more control but requires more maintenance.  ","x":300,"y":13130,"width":500,"height":145},
		{"id":"7c5e8dbb92dbdaf2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a protocol?**|What is a protocol?]]","x":-100,"y":13310,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"7cdd74138ecca30a","type":"text","text":"A `protocol` is a communication method.  \nExamples:\n```\nHTTP\nHTTPS\nTCP\nUDP\nICMP\nSSH\n```\nProtocols define how computers communicate with each other.  \n**Simple VPC example**  \nA common VPC design looks like this:\n```\nVPC: 10.0.0.0/16\n├── Public subnet: 10.0.1.0/24\n│   ├── Web server\n│   └── Load balancer\n│\n├── Private subnet: 10.0.2.0/24\n│   ├── Backend server\n│   └── Database\n│\n├── Internet Gateway\n│   └── Allows public subnet to access the internet\n│\n└── NAT Gateway\n    └── Allows private subnet to access the internet\n```\n**Simple idea**  \nA `VPC` is your private network inside AWS.  \nA `CIDR block` is the IP range of the VPC.  \nA `subnet` is a smaller network inside the VPC.  \nA `route table` controls where traffic goes.  \nAn `Internet Gateway` lets public resources connect to the internet.  \nA `NAT Gateway` lets private resources access the internet safely.  \nFor production, you usually create a custom VPC instead of only using the default VPC.  ","x":300,"y":13310,"width":500,"height":1060},
		{"id":"8a089839c22263f2","type":"text","text":"`Outbound traffic` is traffic going out from your VPC to the internet or another network.  \nExample:\n```\nEC2 instance → Internet\n```","x":300,"y":12342,"width":500,"height":165}
	],
